WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.215 --> 00:00:05.367
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:10.200
In 1949, Harry S Truman was tired.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.230
World War II had just ended, and
in his second inaugural address,

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:20.080
Truman needed to inspire Americans,
weary after years of war.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.240
To do it, he painted America
in a fresh and altruistic way.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.300
He said, and I quote,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:36.060
more than half the world's population
live in conditions approaching misery.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.810
Their economic life is primitive and
stagnant.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.690
We must embark on a bold new program for
the improvement and

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.190
growth of underdeveloped areas.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.270
What Truman is saying here, is a powerful
new idea in the world, development.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.017
If the West pulls our resources,

00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:02.230
we can help poor developing
countries do just that.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.230
Develop, get richer.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.580
It's an uplifting narrative making
Americans feel good that we can help.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.230
One small issue this was a total PR play.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:21.660
It was a concept made up ten days prior
by a mid level State Department employee

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.860
whose only mandate was to reignite
American energy for Truman second term.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.810
At the time of his speech,

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.790
not one document existed to support
the actual execution of the plan.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.098
And to put this in historical context,

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:44.348
a western-led narrative at the time
of benevolence, was unprecedented.

00:01:44.348 --> 00:01:49.494
Western-led colonialism was still
going on after World War II at

00:01:49.494 --> 00:01:56.240
the time of the speech but Americans loved
the new narrative and we followed through.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.859
And today the West, mainly US and

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:06.734
Europe give on average 130
billion in aid per year.

00:02:06.734 --> 00:02:10.770
This is through large philanthropies
like the Gates Foundation,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:14.156
through multi laterals like
the World Bank and IMF, and

00:02:14.156 --> 00:02:18.349
through traditional government aid
like USAID or defit, in the UK.

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:20.581
And through our collective giving,

00:02:20.581 --> 00:02:23.540
Truman's narrative has
stood the test of time.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:32.090
Rich countries help poor countries,
it's something we all kind of know.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:36.640
But for this idea to have legs, we need
to believe that it's in some way working,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.270
that that 130 billion we give in aid
per year is having real impact and

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.110
the poor are getting less poor.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.660
And that too is a widely touted narrative.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.660
In 2015,
the UN published a landmark report

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:58.630
that said that the global poverty rate has
been cut in half over the last 30 years.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.180
And that sounds great because that
means that our efforts are working and

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.810
our global economic system is sound.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.950
And we should all feel good about
that if not for a difficult fact.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:22.710
Global poverty isn't getting better,
in fact, it hasn't budged in 30 years.

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:29.490
So the 2015 claim of huge
wins against global poverty

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.450
relies on an antiquated
$2-a-day poverty line.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.680
That poverty line is set by the same
institutions responsible for

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.380
showing progress against poverty,
and so that line is abysmally low.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.280
Most development economists today cite
that it takes closer to an $8-a-day

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.890
poverty line to achieve basic nutrition
and normal human life expectancy.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.251
And if we look at the $8-a-day
poverty line, and

00:03:57.251 --> 00:04:01.593
subtract out China, which has
taken its own development efforts,

00:04:01.593 --> 00:04:06.468
mostly outside of US control, we see
that the proportion of people living in

00:04:06.468 --> 00:04:10.538
extreme poverty hasn't
meaningfully changed in 3 decades.

00:04:10.538 --> 00:04:14.400
And we can see this reflected again in
our ever growing global inequality.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.490
The gap in GDP per capita between rich and

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.980
poor countries has tripled since
the time of Truman speech.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:25.870
Let that sink in.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:31.690
This is not a tale of poor
countries catching up to the west,

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:35.360
this is a story of
a growing poverty divide.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.910
For me,
this is a profound thing to grapple with.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.320
I work in development economics, and
I've worked to expand access to credit for

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.050
the unbanked in emerging markets.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.850
I lived between Peru and
Indonesia and Kenya and

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.030
I thought I was part of a wave of
things generally getting better.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:02.310
The idea that global poverty is not
getting better hits me to my core

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.520
and it forces me to ask my
trillion dollar question.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:11.010
In the face of all of
these development efforts,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.280
why aren't we making
progress against poverty?

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:23.040
And in that, is the Western influence
I'm a part of helping or hurting?

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.870
My favorite development economist
Jason Hickel has a shocking idea.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:33.166
What if aid, all the bridges,
all the bed nuts is irrelevant or

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:36.000
worse than irrelevant?

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.380
What if it is nothing more
than a convincing PR play?

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:44.009
Like Truman's speech, a distraction.

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:47.080
In this narrative,
we get to be the good guys.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.230
The West is helping the poor catch up and

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.980
everyone gets to sleep at night knowing we
are doing what we can and we don't have

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.141
to do anything differently, besides maybe
give a little bit more in aid per year.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.130
If our global economic system
is working to alleviate poverty,

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.950
but clearly, it's not working.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.530
So what's going on here?

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:17.693
Why haven't we made progress against
poverty despite half a century of effort?

00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:21.330
I want to put that $130 billion
in aid per year in perspective.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:23.470
That's a big number.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.640
But it's much smaller than
the value that flows in

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.197
the opposite direction from poor to rich.

00:06:32.197 --> 00:06:37.026
In 2016, the Global Financial Integrity
Group published shocking data on

00:06:37.026 --> 00:06:41.210
the ways that value is extracted
out of poor countries.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.110
This is not trade flows but

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.940
ways that value is being transferred
sometimes illegally, from poor to rich.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.160
So when Western governments give
loans to poor countries to the IMF,

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.020
we demand back substantial interest rate,
exorbitant interest rates.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.210
And with the miracle of compound interest,

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.890
many poor countries are continuously
crippled under their debt burden.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:11.570
Some of these debts were taken decades ago
and the principle has been long repaid but

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:13.230
we keep demanding back interest.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:16.870
To put his debts in perspective,

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:21.720
the amount that poor countries spend
on debt service each year is a bigger

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.450
number than the UN projects as needed to
eradicate global poverty, completely.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:32.980
And when we loan, on top of interest
we have structural adjustment.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:36.650
So that's when Western governments
lend to poor countries,

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.920
we demand the poor countries
adopt certain economic policies.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.860
And we tell them these policies
will help them develop but really,

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.030
these are policies of free trade and

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:50.930
low tariffs, that benefit our
Western corporations working there.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:56.610
And most nefariously of all,
we have trade misinvoicing,

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:02.300
which is the very illegal yet very
prevalent practice of large corporations,

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:08.020
misreporting trade prices on our invoices
in order to avoid local government taxes.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:14.090
There's a lot more ways the value
flows from poor to rich but

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:18.320
the point here is that our aid
budget is ridiculously small

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.878
compared to all the ways money is
gushing out of poor countries.

00:08:21.878 --> 00:08:27.350
This is outrageous

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:32.010
especially because we've used our
tiny aid budget to fuel a cultural

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:36.810
narrative of our own benevolence and
that narrative hides

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:41.190
the patterns of extraction that
are actively keeping poor countries poor.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.510
Maybe this isn't all that
surprising given centuries

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.400
of Western led extraction
under colonialism.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:55.308
I would argue it's the same
extraction hidden behind a PR

00:08:55.308 --> 00:08:59.110
band-aid of development.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:03.620
I work in development, so
seeing these numbers is deeply troubling.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:09.750
And to be clear, I don't think
development in and of itself is bad, but

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.020
it shouldn't be used as a cover-up for

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.358
extractive global economic
system imposed by the West.

00:09:15.358 --> 00:09:20.270
And I've realized I need to get
my head up out of the sand and

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.770
look at this straight on if
I want to do any good and

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.510
if you want to join me,
there's places to start.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:35.790
Large companies give lip service to
things like ESG through impact and

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.310
CSR, but
I think we need a new code of ethics.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:45.990
So when you run large corporations in
the developing world, do basic things,

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:51.070
like pay your fair share of taxes and
demand the companies you work for do too.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:57.460
And for all of us in the broader West,
I want to challenge us to rethink our role

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:02.370
in development and stop hiding behind
a narrative that the West is helping.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:10.280
To be really blunt, if you're from
a rich country, your privilege was built

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:14.910
on the backs of those in poor countries
and continues to be built on them.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.390
We have to get our story straight.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.470
Which countries aren't
developing poor countries?

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.263
It's the other way around.

00:10:26.263 --> 00:10:26.919
Thank you.

00:10:26.919 --> 00:10:28.838
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:28.838 --> 00:10:33.579
[MUSIC]

